AGENT_TASKS=[{'task_id': 't10', 'instance_id': 'pydicom__pydicom.7d361b3d.pr_1859', 'repo': 'pydicom/pydicom', 'base_commit': 'b2090318825c4026492cd8f19399dffe1923fd14', 'problem_statement': '### NameError: HTJ2KLossless is not defined\n\n**Description**:\nEncountered an issue where the `HTJ2KLossless` UID is not recognized, leading to a `NameError`. This seems to occur during the import process of the `pydicom` library, specifically when handling pixel data.\n\n**Steps to Reproduce**:\n1. Ensure you have the latest version of the `pydicom` library.\n2. Attempt to import the `pydicom` module in a Python script or interactive session:\n   ```python\n   import pydicom\n   ```\n3. Observe the error message indicating that `HTJ2KLossless` is not defined.\n\n**Expected Behavior**:\nThe `pydicom` module should import without any errors, and all UIDs should be properly defined and accessible.\n\n**Environment**:\n- Python version: [Your Python version]\n- pydicom version: [Your pydicom version]\n- Operating System: [Your OS]\n\n**Additional Information**:\nThis issue may be related to recent changes in the UID definitions. Please verify if `HTJ2KLossless` is correctly defined in the `uid.py` file.'}, {'task_id': 't11', 'instance_id': 'pydicom__pydicom.7d361b3d.pr_1995', 'repo': 'pydicom/pydicom', 'base_commit': 'f6f2f4a27d6aaefbdd526b04a5b1a1de51293647', 'problem_statement': 'Code comparison fails when using SRT scheme designator with unmapped values\n\nDescription\n\nWhen comparing two Code objects where one or both have the scheme_designator "SRT" but with values that don\'t exist in the snomed_mapping dictionary, a KeyError is raised instead of properly comparing the codes.\n\nGiven the following code:\n\n```python\nfrom pydicom.sr.coding import Code\n\n# Create two codes with SRT scheme_designator but with values not in the mapping\ncode1 = Code(value="123.456", scheme_designator="SRT", meaning="Test Code 1")\ncode2 = Code(value="789.012", scheme_designator="SRT", meaning="Test Code 2")\n\n# This comparison raises a KeyError\nresult = code1 == code2\n```\n\nThe comparison fails with:\n\n```\nKeyError: \'123.456\'\n```\n\nSteps to reproduce:\n\n1. Create two Code objects with scheme_designator="SRT"\n2. Use values that don\'t exist in the snomed_mapping dictionary\n3. Try to compare the two codes with == or !=\n\nExpected behavior:\nThe comparison should return False for different codes without raising an exception, even if the values don\'t exist in the snomed_mapping dictionary.\n\nActual behavior:\nA KeyError is raised when trying to access a non-existent key in the snomed_mapping dictionary.\n\nThis issue affects any code that compares Code objects with SRT scheme designator where the values aren\'t in the mapping dictionary.\n\n<END WRITING>'}, {'task_id': 't12', 'instance_id': 'pydicom__pydicom.7d361b3d.pr_1987', 'repo': 'pydicom/pydicom', 'base_commit': '8efbb6a346e98c61b96e809a51c3cf9caa4afd33', 'problem_statement': "DataElement.__repr__ inconsistency for SQ (sequence) elements\n\nDescription\n\nI noticed an issue with the representation of DataElement objects that have a value representation (VR) of SQ (sequence). The `__repr__` method for DataElement objects with SQ VR is not behaving as expected.\n\nWhen I create a DataElement with a sequence and try to get its representation, I'm getting inconsistent behavior:\n\n```python\nfrom pydicom.dataelem import DataElement\nfrom pydicom.dataset import Dataset\n\n# Create a sequence element\nelem = DataElement(805961904, 'SQ', [Dataset()])\nelem[0].PatientID = '1234'\n\n# These should be the same but they're not\nprint(repr(elem))\nprint(str(elem))\n```\n\nThe `repr(elem)` is returning a different value than `str(elem)`, which is unexpected. According to the documentation and previous behavior, these should return the same value for sequence elements.\n\nI think this is happening because there are now two implementations of `__repr__` in the DataElement class, and they're handling sequence elements differently. This could cause issues for code that relies on the consistent behavior of `repr()` for DataElement objects.\n\nTo reproduce:\n1. Create a DataElement with a sequence value\n2. Add some data to the sequence\n3. Compare the output of `repr(elem)` and `str(elem)`\n\nExpected behavior:\n- `repr(elem)` and `str(elem)` should return the same string for sequence elements\n\nActual behavior:\n- `repr(elem)` returns a different value than `str(elem)` for sequence elements"}]
